.TH CHMAP_GET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_get \- get value by key (type-inferred, returns value)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

value = chmap_get(hm_name, key);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Type-inferred wrapper for chmap_get_elem_ref() that returns the value itself.
Calls ccol_fatal_err() if the key is not found or the sizes do not match.

The lookup key goes through the DECLARED key type of the map, in the
same way as in chmap_insert(3), so a key that the map stored through
that conversion is only ever found again by a lookup that makes the
same conversion. Both a literal and a computed expression are accepted.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Hash map to search
key      Key to look up
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the value associated with key
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program when the map does not hold the key
Stops the program with ccol_invalid_args when key is a NULL character
pointer, which is not a string
Stops the program when the size of the stored value does not match
the size of the declared value type
Returns the value, not a pointer to it
For a string, returns the char* itself
For a float or double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's
key equality contract (bitwise, except -0.0 canonicalized to 0.0)
For a long double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's key
equality contract (value-based, unlike every other key type)
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_construct(map, int, char*);
int key = 42;
chmap_insert(map, key, "hello");
char* val = chmap_get(map, key); // val is "hello"
chmap_destroy(map);  // val points into the map, so do not use val after this
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_get_ptr (3),
.BR chmap_insert (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3)
